    "body": "US President Donald J. Trump meeting with Chinese President Xi Jinping in China on May 14, 2026. China may lose minerals market share by 2030, CRU says. Credit: The White House | X\nPosted By: Northern Miner Staff November 24, 2026\nChina will keep tighter rules on U.S.-bound shipments of gallium, germanium and antimony on hold for two more months, through Jan. 27, 2027, the Commerce Ministry said Tuesday.\nThe existing suspension was due to expire Nov. 27. The December 2024 rules generally bar export licences for U.S.-bound dual-use items involving the three metals or superhard materials. Dual-use items have both civilian and military applications.\nThe extension also covers stricter checks on buyers and uses of certain U.S.-bound graphite products. China still requires export licences for controlled products and bars exports of dual-use goods to U.S. military users or for U.S. military use.\nChina produced an estimated 99% of the world’s primary low-purity gallium in 2025. U.S. net import reliance for the metal was 100%, and integrated circuits accounted for 73% of U.S. gallium consumption, according to the U.S. Geological Survey.\nRio Tinto (LSE, ASX, NYSE: RIO) and Indium Corp. first recovered gallium at Indium’s research facility in Rome, N.Y., in May 2025, using material from Rio’s Vaudreuil alumina refinery in Quebec. Rio expects a pilot plant at its Saguenay complex to start operating in 2027. A planned demonstration plant at the same site could produce up to 4 tonnes a year.\nSeparately, Beijing has suspended its October 2025 controls on certain rare earths and related technology through Jan. 10, 2027. Licensing rules imposed in April 2025 on shipments of certain rare earths and magnets remain in force.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Rare Earths\nREGION: United States\nCOMPANY: Rio Tinto\nRepublish this article",
